# Untitled

Redox-Active Mixed-Linker Metal–Organic Frameworks with Switchable Semiconductive Characteristics for Tailorable Chemiresistive Sensing
## Introduction
Metal–organic frameworks (MOFs) are crystalline porous materials composed of metal ions or clusters and organic linkers.[1] They possess the advantages of high surface areas, tunable pore environments and designable structures, leading to broad application prospects in gas storage,[2] separation,[3] catalysis,[4] energy storage,[5] magnetism,[6] and sensing.[7] In particular, the chemical and physical properties of MOFs can be rationally designed by judiciously selecting metal nodes and organic linkers.[8] The development of mixed-linker MOFs further enriched the structure and functions of the materials. The combination of multiple functional components in a mixed-linker MOF has been
shown to bring new properties that are beyond the linear combination of the single components.[9] For example, Yaghi and co-workers incorporated up to eight different linkers into a MOF-5 structure to form multivariate MOFs with \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) uptake higher than single-linker-based analogs.[10] Later research demonstrated that the selectivity toward different gases (including \(\mathrm{CH}_{4}\), \(\mathrm{C}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{2}\), and \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\)) can be systematically optimized in mixed-linker MOFs, highlighting the synergistic effects between functional linkers.[11] Mixed-linker MOFs are considered promising candidates for chemical sensing.[12] First, their intrinsic porous structure will allow fast mass transfer and ensure close contact between the analyte and MOFs to increase the sensitivity. Second, the selectivity toward different analytes can be adjusted by different functional groups anchoring on the pore. In addition, the electronic structures and conductivity of mixed-linker MOFs can be modified to optimize chemical sensing performance. Regardless of these advantages, the performance of most MOF-based sensors is limited by the intrinsically poor conductivity of MOFs.
Redox-active metal-bis(dithiolene) and tetrathiafulvalene moieties have been introduced into MOFs to increase their conductivity. For example, the Dincă group synthesized a series of electrically conductive MOFs from tetrathiafulvalene-based linkers with conducting channels formed by S--S interactions between neighboring tetrathiafulvalenes.[13] The electrical conductivity was shown to correlate to the S--S distances.[14] Furthermore, Feng and co-workers constructed conductive MOFs with metal-bis(dithiolene), which showed electrical conductivity up to \(10\,\mathrm{S\,cm^{-1}}\).[15] Both metal-bis(dithiolene) complexes and tetrathiafulvalene-based molecules have rich electrochemical properties and unique optoelectronic properties.[16] Generally, the nickel-bis(dithiolene) motif can be viewed as an inorganic analog of tetrathiafulvalene. They have similar geometries, which led to the formation of isostructural MOFs.[17] However,
they differ significantly in physicochemical properties. Specifically, tetrathiafulvalene is a common donor moiety with multistep reversible oxidation states,[18] while Ni-bis(dithiolene) is more electron-deficient than tetrathiafulvalene. As a result, tetrathiafulvalene and its derivatives are known as p-type semiconductors,[19] whereas Ni-bis(dithiolene) complexes have been documented as n-type semiconductors.[20] The different physicochemical properties of tetrathiafulvalene and Ni-bis(dithiolene) moieties are expected to result in distinct sensing behaviors. For example, chemical sensors based on p-type semiconductors are usually more sensitive toward volatile organic compounds (VOCs), while n-type semiconductors favor the adsorption of oxidizing gases such as \(\mathrm{NO}_{2}\).[19] The similar size and different electronic properties of tetrathiafulvalene and Ni-bis(dithiolene) inspired us to simultaneously incorporate two components into a mixed-linker MOF and systematically study their effects on the chemiresistive sensing of VOCs.
Herein, a series of mixed-linker MOFs, namely \(\mathbf{M}\mathbf{n}_{2}\) - \([\mathrm{TTF}]_{x}[\mathrm{NiS}_{4}]_{1-x}\) \((0\!\le\!x\!\le\!1)\) , were synthesized using tetrathiafulvalene tetrabenzoic acid (abbreviated as [TTF]) and Ni-bis[1,2-di(4-carboxylphenyl)-1,2-dithiolene] (abbreviated as \([\mathrm{NiS}_{4}])\) linkers of various ratios, which represent a rare example of mixed-linker MOF system with both organic linkers and organometallic linkers. Interestingly, a switching of the semiconducting behaviors from n-type to p-type was observed for \(\mathrm{Mn}_{2}[\mathrm{TTF}]_{x}[\mathrm{NiS}_{4}]_{1-x}\) \((0\!\le\!x\!\le\!1)\) as the tetrathiafulvalene ratio increased above \(78\,\%\) . To our knowledge, this is the first example of \(\mathfrak{n}{-}/\mathfrak{p}\) - switching in electrically conductive MOF materials. The \(\mathfrak{n}{-}\mathfrak{p}\) - switching is accompanied by the continuous conductivity change from \(2.88\times\) \(10^{-7}\;\mathrm{S}\,\mathrm{m}^{-1}\) to \(9.26\!\times\!10^{-5}\,\mathrm{S}\,\mathrm{m}^{-1}\) by varying the linker ratio. \(\mathrm{Mn}_{2}[\mathrm{TTF}]_{x}[\mathrm{NiS}_{4}]_{1-x}\) series were further fabricated into electronic gas sensors and evaluated for VOC detection under different conditions. Among the \(\mathrm{Mn}_{2}[\mathrm{TTF}]_{x}[\mathrm{NiS}_{4}]_{1-x}\) \((0\!\le\!x\!\le\) 1) series, \(\mathrm{Mn}_{2}[\mathrm{TTF}]_{0.78}[\mathrm{NiS}_{4}]_{0.22}\) showed the highest sensitivity to ethanol \((5\,\mathrm{ppm})\) and acetone \((20\,\mathrm{ppm})\) vapors, which is higher than those of single-linker based analogs, highlighting the synergistic effect of mixed-linker MOFs. On the other hand, \(\mathrm{Mn}_{2}[\mathrm{NiS}_{4}]\) exhibited the most rapid response/recovery and highest humidity resistance. This work provides a tunable MOF platform for the development of high-performance and multi-scenario chemiresistive sensors.
## Results and Discussion
To incorporate tetrathiafulvalene and Ni-bis(dithiolene) moieties into MOFs, two linkers with phenylcarboxylate groups on the tetrathiafulvalene and Ni-bis(dithiolene) centers were selected, namely, [TTF] and \([\mathrm{NiS}_{4}]\) . They show the same tetragonal geometry and similar sizes based on single crystal data (8.76 and \(14.85\,\mathrm{\AA}\) for [TTF]; 8.49 and \(14.80\,\mathrm{\AA}\) for \([\mathrm{NiS}_{4}]\) by measuring the distances between the C atoms of adjacent carboxyl groups, Figure S1).[14] Therefore, we expect that two linkers can be incorporated simultaneously to form a mixed-linker MOF. \(\mathrm{Mn}_{2}[\mathrm{TTF}]\) ,[14] a previously reported MOF based on \(\mathbf{M}\mathbf{n}^{2+}\) and \([\mathrm{TTF}]^{4-}\)
linkers, was selected as a matrix to accommodate two linkers because of its relatively good conductivity originating from the S--S interaction between stacked linkers. We expect that [TTF] and \([\mathrm{NiS}_{4}]\) can form mixed linker MOFs in which the electronic properties can be controlled by varying linker ratios. As expected, dark rod-like crystals of mixed-linker MOFs containing [TTF] and \([\mathrm{NiS}_{4}]\) were successfully obtained by solvothermal reactions, namely \(\mathrm{Mn}_{2}[\mathrm{TTF}]_{x}[\mathrm{NiS}_{4}]_{1-x}\) \((0\!\le\!x\!\le\!1)\) , where \(x\) represents the molar ratio of [TTF] determined by inductively coupled plasma atomic emission spectroscopy (ICP-AES). The detailed analysis of linker ratios will be discussed in later sections.
\(\mathrm{Mn}_{2}[\mathrm{TTF}]_{x}[\mathrm{NiS}_{4}]_{1-x}\) \((x\!=\!1\) , 0.78, 0.49, 0.24, and 0) with variable linker ratios were successfully synthesized. Powder X-ray diffraction (PXRD) of samples with different molar ratios of [TTF] and \([\mathrm{NiS}_{4}]\) showed similar peak positions (Figure 2a), suggesting that the mixed-linker MOFs exhibit the same structure as the single-linker-based MOFs. However, the intensity of the (100) diffraction peak at 5.3 degrees increases with more \([\mathrm{NiS}_{4}]\) linkers, which can be related to the higher electron density contrast of the (100) lattice plane caused by Ni atoms. This intensity difference in the (100) diffraction peak is also consistent with the simulated PXRD patterns based on the respective single-crystal structures (Figure 2a gray curves).
The different linker ratios can be directly observed by the color of the samples from red to dark green (Figure 2b). Elemental mapping by scanning electron microscopy (SEM) shows the uniform distribution of Mn, S, and Ni elements throughout different scale particles of \(\mathrm{Mn}_{2}[\mathrm{TTF}]_{0.49}[\mathrm{NiS}_{4}]_{0.51}\) (Figures 2c and S5). These results imply that tetrathiafulva
lene and Ni-bis(dithiolene) are uniformly distributed throughout the crystals of mixed-linker \(\mathrm{Mn}_{2}[\mathrm{TTF}]_{0.49}.\) - \([\mathrm{NiS}_{4}]_{0.51}\) . Fourier-transform infrared spectra (FT-IR) of \(\mathrm{Mn}_{2}[\mathrm{TTF}]_{x}[\mathrm{NiS}_{4}]_{1-x}\) \((x\!=\!1,\,0.78,\,0.49,\,0.24,\) and 0) are similar except for the peaks at \(1352\,\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) , which correspond to the Ni-bis(dithiolene) moieties. The gradual growth of the \(1352\,\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) peak indicated the increased concentration of \([\mathrm{NiS}_{4}]\) in the mixed-linker MOFs (Figure S6). Similar to FTIR, Raman spectra of \(\mathrm{Mn}_{2}[\mathrm{TTF}]_{x}[\mathrm{NiS}_{4}]_{1-x}\) \((x\!=\!1,\,0.78,\,0.49,\)
0.24, and 0) also show increased peak intensity at \(380\;\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) , in line with the higher ratio of \([\mathrm{NiS}_{4}]\) (Figure S7).[23]
Since the \(^1\mathrm{H}\,\mathrm{NMR}\) peaks of [TTF] and \([\mathrm{NiS}_{4}]\) linkers overlap with each other,[13,17] we used ICP-AES to determine the linker ratios in the mixed-linker MOFs. The linker ratios were calculated from the Mn and Ni elements based on the formula of \(\mathrm{Mn}_{2}[\mathrm{TTF}]_{x}[\mathrm{NiS}_{4}]_{1-x}\) . The Mn to Ni ratios were measured to be 9.09, 3.92, and 2.63, respectively (Table S3), corresponding to formulas of \(\mathrm{Mn}_{2}[\mathrm{TTF}]_{0.78}[\mathrm{NiS}_{4}]_{0.22}\) , \(\mathrm{Mn}_{2}.\) - \([\mathrm{TTF}]_{0.49}[\mathrm{NiS}_{4}]_{0.51}\) , and \(\mathrm{Mn}_{2}[\mathrm{TTF}]_{0.24}[\mathrm{NiS}_{4}]_{0.76}\) (Figure S8). The [TTF] to \([\mathrm{NiS}_{4}]\) ratio determined by ICP (0.49:0.51) also matches well with the EDX (0.46:0.54) (Figures S9 and S10) and SC-XRD (0.48:0.52) results of \(\mathrm{Mn}_{2}[\mathrm{TTF}]_{0.49}[\mathrm{NiS}_{4}]_{0.51}\) .
The mixed-linker \(\mathrm{Mn}_{2}[\mathrm{TTF}]_{x}[\mathrm{NiS}_{4}]_{1-x}\) \((x\!=\!1,\,0.78,\,0.49,\) 0.24, 0) series possess porous structures, as revealed by the \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) adsorption measurements at \(77\,\mathrm{K}\) . All the samples show the type I isotherm, indicating microporous structures (Figure 2d). The pore size was estimated to be \(0.65\;\mathrm{nm}\) , which is consistent with the one-dimensional channels \((0.74\,\mathrm{nm})\) in single-crystal structures (Figure S11). The Brunauer–Emmett–Teller (BET) surface areas of \(\mathrm{Mn}_{2}[\mathrm{TTF}]_{x}[\mathrm{NiS}_{4}]_{1-x}\) \((x\!=\) 1, 0.78, 0.49, 0.24, and 0) were calculated to be 528, 433, 451, 431 and \(398\,\mathrm{m}^{2}\,\mathrm{g}^{-1}\) , respectively. The high BET surface area \((528{-}398\;\mathrm{m}^{2}\,\mathrm{g}^{-1})\) and uniform pore size \((0.65\;\mathrm{nm})\) ensure the fast diffusion of VOC analytes (kinetic diameters of 0.46 for acetone and \(0.45\,\mathrm{nm}\) for ethanol).
To evaluate the stability of mixed-linker MOFs under the condition of VOC detection, the PXRD of mixed-linker MOFs in different solvents was studied. Taking \(\mathrm{Mn}_{2}.\) - \([\mathrm{TTF}]_{0.49}[\mathrm{NiS}_{4}]_{0.51}\) as an example, it maintains crystallinity in ethanol, acetone, and saturated water vapor for \(24\,\mathrm{{h}}\) (Figure 2e). Thermogravimetric analysis (TGA) further shows the good thermal stability of \(\mathrm{Mn}_{2}[\mathrm{TTF}]_{x}[\mathrm{NiS}_{4}]_{1-x}\) up to \(350\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) , beyond which framework collapse occurs (Figure S12). The fairly good chemical and thermal stability of the mixed-linker \(\mathrm{Mn}_{2}[\mathrm{TTF}]_{x}[\mathrm{NiS}_{4}]_{1-x}\) series ensure their application in chemical sensing.
The electrical conductivity of mixed-linker MOFs is expected to have a strong impact on the chemiresistive sensing performance. Therefore, we investigated the electrical conductivity of \(\mathrm{Mn}_{2}[\mathrm{TTF}]_{x}[\mathrm{NiS}_{4}]_{1-x}\) powder compacts
using the two-probe method. The electrical conductivities of \(\mathrm{Mn}_{2}[\mathrm{TTF}]\), \(\mathrm{Mn}_{2}[\mathrm{TTF}]_{0.78}[\mathrm{NiS}_{4}]_{0.22}\), \(\mathrm{Mn}_{2}[\mathrm{TTF}]_{0.49}[\mathrm{NiS}_{4}]_{0.51}\), \(\mathrm{Mn}_{2}[\mathrm{TTF}]_{0.24}[\mathrm{NiS}_{4}]_{0.76}\), and \(\mathrm{Mn}_{2}[\mathrm{NiS}_{4}]\) were measured to be \(9.26 \times 10^{-5}\), \(8.17 \times 10^{-5}\), \(7.38 \times 10^{-5}\), \(1.50 \times 10^{-5}\) and \(2.88 \times 10^{-7} \, \mathrm{S \, m^{-1}}\) at room temperature, respectively (Table S4). A roughly linear correlation is observed between the conductivity and the linker ratio (Figures S13 and S14), where \(\mathrm{Mn}_{2}[\mathrm{TTF}]\) shows the highest conductivity. This is tentatively explained by the higher degree of electron delocalization of [TTF], resulting in better conductivity. In addition, the shorter S\(\cdots\)S distance between adjacent linkers in \(\mathrm{Mn}_{2}[\mathrm{TTF}]\) than in \(\mathrm{Mn}_{2}[\mathrm{TTF}]_{0.49}[\mathrm{NiS}_{4}]_{0.51}\) and \(\mathrm{Mn}_{2}[\mathrm{NiS}_{4}]\) may also contribute to the conductivity of \(\mathrm{Mn}_{2}[\mathrm{TTF}]\). Therefore, the electrical conductivity of \(\mathrm{Mn}_{2}[\mathrm{TTF}]_{x}[\mathrm{NiS}_{4}]_{1-x}\) can be modulated by three orders of magnitude by controlling the linker ratios.
In addition to the electrical conductivity, the majority charge carriers and semiconducting behaviors of a conductive MOF are generally critical to the corresponding applications. It can be observed that \(\mathrm{Mn}_{2}[\mathrm{TTF}]_{x}[\mathrm{NiS}_{4}]_{1-x}\) switch from p-type to n-type behavior with increased \([\mathrm{NiS}_{4}]\) linker ratios. To elucidate the features in electronic structures of \(\mathrm{Mn}_{2}[\mathrm{TTF}]_{x}[\mathrm{NiS}_{4}]_{1-x}\), a series of Mott–Schottky tests were performed at 500, 750 and \(1000 \, \mathrm{Hz}\) (Figures 3a–c). In the Mott–Schottky plot of \(\mathrm{Mn}_{2}[\mathrm{TTF}]\), the \(\mathrm{C}^{-2}\) value has a negative slope to the potential, while the intersection with the X-axis is independent of frequency. These results reveal that \(\mathrm{Mn}_{2}[\mathrm{TTF}]\) is a typical p-type semiconductor[24] with a flat-band potential of \(0.01 \, \mathrm{V}\) vs. \(\mathrm{Ag/AgCl}\). \(\mathrm{Mn}_{2}[\mathrm{TTF}]_{0.78}[\mathrm{NiS}_{4}]_{0.22}\) shows a Mott–Schottky plot similar to that of \(\mathrm{Mn}_{2}[\mathrm{TTF}]\) except for the higher \(\mathrm{C}^{-2}\) value, which is tentatively attributed to the decreased concentration of donors. When the \([\mathrm{NiS}_{4}]\) ratio increases to \(51\,\%\) in \(\mathrm{Mn}_{2}[\mathrm{TTF}]_{0.49}[\mathrm{NiS}_{4}]_{0.51}\), the slope of \(\mathrm{C}^{-2}\) vs. potential turns positive (Figure 3c), indicating a transition to an n-type semiconduc
tor with a flat-band potential at \(-0.62 \, \mathrm{V}\) vs. \(\mathrm{Ag/AgCl}\).[25] Further increasing the \([\mathrm{NiS}_{4}]\) concentration does not alter the n-type semiconducting behavior. The semiconductor behavior can be further confirmed by photocurrent and photovoltage responses (Figure S15). When \(\mathrm{Mn}_{2}[\mathrm{TTF}]\) was irradiated by visible light \((150 \, \mathrm{mW \, cm}^{-2})\), an obvious cathodic photocurrent of \(\approx 10 \, \mathrm{nA \, cm}^{-2}\) (Figure 3d) was observed. The positive photovoltage response (Figure S16) at open circuit potential further verifies its p-type characteristics.[24] The transition from p-type to n-type behavior with increasing \([\mathrm{NiS}_{4}]\) ratio is reflected by the opposite photocurrent and photovoltage responses (Figure S17). The transition of the semiconductor type will have a clear impact on the sensing performance based on the charge transfer mechanism, which will be discussed in the next section.
Benefiting from the unique ligand tunability and resulting electronic structure variations of \(\mathrm{Mn}_{2}[\mathrm{TTF}]_{x}[\mathrm{NiS}_{4}]_{1-x}\), the corresponding materials are expected to enable continuous and precise control of the chemiresistive sensing performance for VOC detection. Microcrystalline samples of \(\mathrm{Mn}_{2}[\mathrm{TTF}]_{x}[\mathrm{NiS}_{4}]_{1-x}\) with particle widths of \(\approx 1 \, \upmu \mathrm{m}\) were prepared by lowering the reaction temperature, which are suitable for the fabrication of thin-film devices (Figures S18–S20). The gas-sensing performance was tested using a custom-made sensing chamber system (Figure S21) developed in our previous studies.[26] Figures 4a and S22 illustrate the structure of a chemiresistive sensory device fabricated from the thin film of \(\mathrm{Mn}_{2}[\mathrm{TTF}]_{x}[\mathrm{NiS}_{4}]_{1-x}\) MOFs. In a typical sensing test, a specific concentration of VOC analyte diluted in the carrier gas (dry \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\)) was introduced into the chamber to cause real-time changes in the conductance of the MOF sensors. Figures 4b and c describe the real-time measurement of \(\mathrm{Mn}_{2}[\mathrm{TTF}]_{x}[\mathrm{NiS}_{4}]_{1-x}\) conductivities with different ligand ratios upon exposure to varying concentrations of ethanol and acetone vapors, respectively. The conductance of \(\mathrm{Mn}_{2}[\mathrm{TTF}]\) shows a significant decrease upon introducing VOCs, indicating a typical p-type semiconductor sensing characteristic.[7a,24] When the ratio of \([\mathrm{NiS}_{4}]\) increases to \(22\,\%\), the relative conductance response from \(\mathrm{Mn}_{2}[\mathrm{TTF}]_{0.78}[\mathrm{NiS}_{4}]_{0.22}\) demonstrates a significant enhancement (approximately an order of magnitude) compared to \(\mathrm{Mn}_{2}[\mathrm{TTF}]\). Interestingly, when further increasing the ratio of the \([\mathrm{NiS}_{4}]\) ligand to \(51\,\%\), \(\mathrm{Mn}_{2}[\mathrm{TTF}]_{x}[\mathrm{NiS}_{4}]_{1-x}\) \((x=0.49\), 0.24 and 0) switch to a positive signal toward VOCs, indicating a p-type to n-type conversion, as shown in Figure 4b and c.
Specifically in the detection of ethanol, the relative conductance change \((\Delta G/G_{0})\) vs. ethanol concentration calibration curves of \(\mathrm{Mn}_{2}[\mathrm{TTF}]_{x}[\mathrm{NiS}_{4}]_{1-x}\) series all show good linearity in the tested range, as shown in Figure 4d. It can be concluded that the gradually increasing percentage of \([\mathrm{NiS}_{4}]\) leads to a higher level of relative conductance response. In addition, significantly reduced response time and increased recovery level were observed along with an increased \([\mathrm{NiS}_{4}]\) ratio (summarized in Figure 4f). As a result, the response time and recovery time of \(\mathrm{Mn}_{2}[\mathrm{NiS}_{4}]\) for 50 ppm ethanol reach 32.3 s and 23.4 s, respectively, demonstrating the most rapid response/recovery among all MOF-based room-temperature chemiresistors to date, which is
also faster than other reported composite-based chemiresistors (Table S5). The alternation in the p-n characteristics occurs at \(x\!\leq\!0.51\) , accompanied by the continuous evolution of the sensing performances. It should also be noted that the \(\mathrm{Mn}_{2}[\mathrm{TTF}]_{x}[\mathrm{NiS}_{4}]_{1-x}\) sensors followed a typical semiconductor-based charge transfer mechanism toward the detection of VOCs, where electron-donating organic vapors lead to a negative response in p-type \(\mathrm{Mn}_{2}[\mathrm{TTF}]\) and \(\mathrm{Mn}_{2}[\mathrm{TTF}]_{0.78^{-}}\) \([\mathrm{NiS}_{4}]_{0.22}\) , as well as a positive response in n-type \(\mathbf{M}\mathbf{n}_{2}\) - \([\mathrm{TTF}]_{0.49}[\mathrm{NiS}_{4}]_{0.51}\) , \(\mathrm{Mn}_{2}[\mathrm{TTF}]_{0.24}[\mathrm{NiS}_{4}]_{0.76}\) , and \(\mathrm{Mn}_{2}[\mathrm{NiS}_{4}]\) . The sensitivity, limit of detection (LOD), response/recovery, RH resistance and sensor power of the \(\mathrm{Mn}_{2}[\mathrm{TTF}]_{x}[\mathrm{NiS}_{4}]_{1-x}\) series are systematically summarized and compared in Figure 4f. As can be observed from the overall results, the sensing performance of mixed-linker \(\mathrm{Mn}_{2}[\mathrm{TTF}]_{x}[\mathrm{NiS}_{4}]_{1-x}\) does not simply exhibit average values of single-linker-based \(\mathbf{M}\mathbf{n}_{2}\) - [TTF] and \(\mathrm{Mn}_{2}[\mathrm{NiS}_{4}]\) , indicating a nonlinear tailoring of the sensing performance from the synergistic mixed linker effects. Specifically, the mixed-linker \(\mathrm{Mn}_{2}[\mathrm{TTF}]_{0.78}[\mathrm{NiS}_{4}]_{0.22}\) showed the highest signal-to-noise ratios (SNRs, 29.91 to 5 ppm ethanol and 31.36 to \(20\,\mathrm{ppm}\) acetone), which are higher than those of single-linker-based \(\mathrm{Mn}_{2}[\mathrm{TTF}]\) (14.29 to
5 ppm ethanol and 19.48 to \(20\,\mathrm{ppm}\) acetone). The theoretical limits of detection (LODs) were also determined from the extension of calibration curves, and \(\mathrm{Mn}_{2}[\mathrm{TTF}]_{0.78}[\mathrm{NiS}_{4}]_{0.22}\) showed the lowest theoretical LODs (Figure 4f).[27] These results highlight the advantage of mixed-linker MOFs as tunable platforms to realize sensing performances that single-linker-based MOFs cannot achieve.
At the same time, the relative conductance response \((\Delta G/G_{0})\) of \(\mathrm{Mn}_{2}[\mathrm{NiS}_{4}]\) reached \(3481.1\,\%\) for \(100\,\mathrm{ppm}\) ethanol, which is two orders of magnitude higher than that reported for \(\mathrm{Cu}_{3}(\mathrm{HHTPP})_{2}\).[28] To the best of our knowledge, \(\mathrm{Mn}_{2}[\mathrm{TTF}]_{x}[\mathrm{NiS}_{4}]_{1-x}\) exhibits the highest response among the reported MOF sensors,[7a,28,29] as summarized in Figure 4g, which even exceeds most metal oxide-based VOC sensors operating at high temperatures (Table S6).[29e] Furthermore, compared with inorganic semiconducting VOC sensors, \(\mathrm{Mn}_{2}[\mathrm{TTF}]_{x}[\mathrm{NiS}_{4}]_{1-x}\) has extremely low power consumption. As shown in Figure 4f, the power of \(\mathrm{Mn}_{2}[\mathrm{NiS}_{4}]\) is as low as \(0.0153\;\mathrm{nW}\) . Similar to the trend in ethanol response, the response of \(\mathrm{Mn}_{2}[\mathrm{TTF}]_{x}[\mathrm{NiS}_{4}]_{1-x}\) to acetone vapors is also dependent on the linker ratio, which allows us to optimize the sensing performance (Figure S23). To probe the repeat
ability and stability of \(\mathrm{Mn}_{2}[\mathrm{TTF}]_{x}[\mathrm{NiS}_{4}]_{1-x}\) MOFs in VOC detection, repeated sensing tests (to \(200\,\mathrm{ppm}\) acetone) were conducted on \(\mathrm{Mn}_{2}[\mathrm{TTF}]_{0.49}[\mathrm{NiS}_{4}]_{0.51}\) for ten times. As shown in Figure 4e, no obvious fluctuation or attenuation is observed, with a coefficient of variation as low as \(0.78\,\%\). In addition, the X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS), \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) adsorption measurements and PXRD results of the samples are before and after the tests also show that the samples are stable (Figures S24–S26). More importantly, relative humidity (RH) is a highly reactive background vapor that inevitably exists in the environment (such as in diagnostic breath VOC analysis), causing severe cross-sensitivity to chemiresistive VOC detectors. \(\mathrm{Mn}_{2}[\mathrm{TTF}]_{x}[\mathrm{NiS}_{4}]_{1-x}\) MOFs demonstrate different levels of resistance to RH, which is also correlated to the linker ratio (see Figures S27–S32). Here, we quantitatively define RH resistance as the highest RH level that first caused an influential response \((\mathrm{SNR}>3)\) in the MOF sensors. As summarized in Figure 4f, the RH resistance increases from \(60\,\%\) to \(80\,\%\) with increasing \([\mathrm{NiS}_{4}]\) ratio in \(\mathrm{Mn}_{2}[\mathrm{TTF}]_{x}[\mathrm{NiS}_{4}]_{1-x}\). At the same time, even for \(\mathrm{Mn}_{2}[\mathrm{TTF}]\) with the lowest RH resistance, the response to \(50\,\%\) RH was only \(0.0435\,\%\), which is much lower than its response to \(5\,\mathrm{ppm}\) ethanol \((3.343\,\%)\) and \(20\,\mathrm{ppm}\) acetone \((6.785\,\%)\), demonstrating satisfactory VOC selectivity toward RH. Water contact angle (WCA) measurements (Figure S33) elucidate the origin of RH resistance exhibited in \(\mathrm{Mn}_{2}[\mathrm{TTF}]_{x}[\mathrm{NiS}_{4}]_{1-x}\) MOFs. When the ratio of \([\mathrm{NiS}_{4}]\) increases to \(51\,\%\), the WCA value increases from \(71.6^{\circ}\) for \(\mathrm{Mn}_{2}[\mathrm{TTF}]\) to \(77.2^{\circ}\) for \(\mathrm{Mn}_{2}[\mathrm{TTF}]_{0.49}[\mathrm{NiS}_{4}]_{0.51}\), weakening the hydrophilicity of the material. The WCA value of pure \(\mathrm{Mn}_{2}[\mathrm{NiS}_{4}]\) further increases to \(90.4^{\circ}\), indicating a hydrophobic surface. Therefore, \(\mathrm{Mn}_{2}[\mathrm{TTF}]_{x}[\mathrm{NiS}_{4}]_{1-x}\) serves as a tunable platform for the design and optimization of chemiresistive sensors for different application scenarios.
## Conclusion
In summary, we have successfully prepared a series of mixed-linker MOFs with redox-active motifs \(\mathrm{Mn}_{2}[\mathrm{TTF}]_{x}[\mathrm{NiS}_{4}]_{1-x}\) \((0\leq x\leq1)\) and studied the effect of linker ratios on the electronic structures of materials. Interestingly, the variation of linker ratios not only changed the electrical conductivity of MOFs by 3 orders of magnitude, but also induced a switch of n-type/p-type semiconducting behaviors. By adjusting the ratio of the two linkers in MOFs, we have realized the fabrication and optimization of chemiresistive VOC gas sensors for different application scenarios. \(\mathrm{Mn}_{2}[\mathrm{TTF}]_{0.78}[\mathrm{NiS}_{4}]_{0.22}\) is suitable for the detection of low-concentration ethanol and acetone vapors, while \(\mathrm{Mn}_{2}[\mathrm{NiS}_{4}]\) fits the rapid VOC detection under high humidity, such as drunk driving detection or disease diagnosis via exhaled human breath. In addition to providing a versatile material for chemiresistive sensing, this work also highlights the unique advantage of mixed-linker MOFs in the rational design of electro-/opto- devices for targeted applications. Considering the unlimited tunability of MOFs, advanced sensors can be envisioned by adjusting the metal and ligand components, or a combination thereof. Further investiga
tions are currently underway to explore the effect of metal centers on sensing performance for a deeper understanding of structure–activity relationships. Considering the wide applications of tetrathiafulvalene- or nickel-bis(dithiolene)-based materials, the \([\mathrm{TTF}]/[\mathrm{NiS}_{4}]\)-mixed-linker MOFs are expected to find applications in electrochemical sensors, charge storage devices, electrocatalysts, and beyond.
